    "body": "Ibogaine, which operates via a “matrix pharmacology,” has long been a focus of Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. and the Trump administration.\nThe Department of Health and Human Services is full steam ahead on ibogaine. On Thursday, the department announced a range of investments in the psychedelic compound, along with other rapid-acting psychotropic drugs for serious mental illnesses, including post-traumatic stress disorder.\nUp to $4.9 million will be invested through the Advanced Research Projects Agency for Health (ARPA-H) to advance these investigational medicines for PTSD “and other serious mental illnesses, including in veterans,” HHS revealed on Thursday during a Veteran’s Day announcement.\nThis initial commitment, which will be distributed over five years, will be followed by additional awards that will focus on ibogaine as a potential treatment for substance use disorders, according to the HHS press release. The awards, which will “vary in funding amount” per awardee, will go toward the regulatory and manufacturing groundwork for future trials.\n“Veterans kept their promise to this country. We must keep ours,” Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. said in a prepared statement. “Veterans have told us ibogaine changed their lives. Their experiences demand serious scientific investigation. We will pursue the evidence, confront the risks and put affordability at the center of this work.”\nAdditionally, the National Institute of Mental Health is offering a $20 million funding opportunity to launch a research network to study rapid-acting psychotropic interventional drugs, including ketamine, esketamine, psilocybin and other emerging treatments for serious mental illnesses. Johnson & Johnson markets an esketamine nasal spray called Spravato, which was first approved in 2019 and is now cleared for three types of severe depression.\nPsychedelics\nDespite FDA trial clearance, ibogaine metabolite won’t receive red carpet treatment\nDemeRx is launching the first U.S. clinical trial of an ibogaine-derived drug candidate, marking a pivotal moment for a controversial psychedelic long sidelined by safety concerns.\nJune 15, 2026\n·\n10 min read\n·\nGabrielle Masson\nRead more\n“Reports of successful ibogaine treatments are too promising to ignore. We owe it to our heroes and every person struggling to ask the bold questions and deliver results in years, not decades,” Al Bugazia, senior adviser for Special Projects at ARPA-H said in a prepared statement. “As long as people continue to struggle, doing anything less is not an option.”\nIbogaine has been a key focus for the Trump administration. In April, the president issued an executive order to accelerate the development of and facilitate broader access to psychedelic therapies. Ibogaine, which operates via a “matrix pharmacology,” according to Columbia University professor Dalibor Sames, was a key focus of the order .\nIbogaine acts across different molecules in cells, impacting serotonin signaling via a broad transporter network rather than acting on a single target, Sames hypothesized with colleagues in a study published in January.\n“What we found over and over is that ibogaine doesn’t fit into the standard pharmacological scientific paradigms,” the professor told BioSpace in June.\nIn late April, the FDA cleared DemeRx’s oral noribogaine candidate DMX-1001 to begin first-in-human testing. The company plans to kick off a Phase 2 trial in alcohol use disorder next year.\nAlongside its potential benefit, however, ibogaine is also known for its neurotoxic and cardiotoxic effects, which can be associated with severe complications and even death. As of 2022, 33 deaths after ibogaine use had been recorded in published literature .\nThis latest push by HHS comes on the heels of FDA submissions for two psilocybin therapeutics, including the completion of a rolling application for Compass Pathways’ highly anticipated COMP360 for treatment-resistant depression. Compass’ announcement came just a week after a submission from competitor Usona Institute for PSIL201 in major depressive disorder.\nPsychedelics\nHow does ibogaine work? Understanding matrix logic to make a safer drug\nIbogaine’s unconventional “matrix pharmacology” may underlie both its therapeutic promise and unpredictable cardiac risks. Unraveling this mechanism could help drug developers hoping to bring ibogaine analogs to market.\nJune 15, 2026\n·\n3 min read\n·\nGabrielle Masson\nRead more",
